{
  "id": "francarabe_judeo_arabic_song",
  "name": "Francarabe (Judeo-Arabic popular song of the Maghreb)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "North African urban popular song of Algeria and Tunisia recorded from the 1920s into the 1960s, first on Gramophone, Pathé and Baidaphon 78s and later for radio and LP, mixing Arabic and French lyrics and setting hawzi, chaabi and Andalusi-derived melodies to tango, paso doble, rumba and foxtrot rhythms. Many of its leading singers were Algerian and Tunisian Jews who carried the repertoire to Paris after independence; the fiddle card stands for the violin, the hand drum card for the derbouka goblet drum and the credited frame drums card for the jingled tar.",
  "recipe": "classic North African mid-1920s francarabe, functional Song, mid east n African pop branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. walnut ribs canonical mid tier nylon oud, melodic bowed song accompaniment fiddle, block chord upright piano, hand drum source unspecified, credited frame drums. stone plaster medina room. large-diaphragm condenser, German V72 tube pre, German tube console. tape 15ips.",
